{"page":906,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.2516,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"892 Chapter 15: Multiple Integrals"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.09317,"w":0.42932,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 3 Sketch the region of integration for the integral"},{"x":0.05409,"y":0.11923,"w":0.12517,"h":0.01423,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"4 (2, 4)"},{"x":0.58395,"y":0.12011,"w":0.01084,"h":0.00896,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2x"},{"x":0.57659,"y":0.13256,"w":0.11606,"h":0.02476,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"x2 (4x + 2) dy dx"},{"x":0.55317,"y":0.14407,"w":0.02909,"h":0.01356,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"0 L"},{"x":0.08489,"y":0.15278,"w":0.03561,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = 2x"},{"x":0.32334,"y":0.16905,"w":0.45735,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"and write an equivalent integral with the order of integration reversed."},{"x":0.13363,"y":0.17517,"w":0.03463,"h":0.01214,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = x2"},{"x":0.32334,"y":0.19075,"w":0.59299,"h":0.04864,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"Solution The region of integration is given by the inequalities x2 … y … 2x and 0 … x … 2. It is therefore the region bounded by the curves y = x2 and y = 2x between x = 0 and x = 2 (Figure 15.16a)."},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.24195,"w":0.59303,"h":0.04348,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"To find limits for integrating in the reverse order, we imagine a horizontal line passing from left to right through the region. It enters at x = y>2 and leaves at x = 2y. To include all such lines, we let y run from y = 0 to y = 4 (Figure 15.16b). The integral is"},{"x":0.05409,"y":0.25837,"w":0.09767,"h":0.01035,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 2"},{"x":0.14184,"y":0.27412,"w":0.01453,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(a)"},{"x":0.57956,"y":0.30299,"w":0.01597,"h":0.00895,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2y"},{"x":0.59558,"y":0.31544,"w":0.10187,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"(4x + 2) dx dy."},{"x":0.05582,"y":0.31746,"w":0.12573,"h":0.01423,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"4 (2, 4)"},{"x":0.57187,"y":0.32396,"w":0.01578,"h":0.01626,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y>2"},{"x":0.54845,"y":0.32695,"w":0.02909,"h":0.01356,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"0 L"},{"x":0.32334,"y":0.35833,"w":0.27287,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The common value of these integrals is 8."},{"x":0.09427,"y":0.37408,"w":0.00654,"h":0.0236,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"y 2"},{"x":0.07069,"y":0.38114,"w":0.11262,"h":0.0169,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"x = \"y x ="},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.39505,"w":0.22809,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Properties of Double Integrals"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.41971,"w":0.59307,"h":0.02814,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Like single integrals, double integrals of continuous functions have algebraic properties that are useful in computations and applications."},{"x":0.05582,"y":0.4566,"w":0.10438,"h":0.02534,"fs":0.01023,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"0 2 (b)"},{"x":0.35357,"y":0.48923,"w":0.53247,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"If ƒ(x, y) and g(x, y) are continuous on the bounded region R, then the following properties hold."},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.49402,"w":0.1742,"h":0.02762,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 15.16 Region of integration for Example 3."},{"x":0.5317,"y":0.54228,"w":0.10272,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"cƒ(x, y) dA = c"},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.54295,"w":0.17357,"h":0.03037,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"1. Constant Multiple: O R"},{"x":0.66389,"y":0.54295,"w":0.17976,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(x, y) dA (any number c)"},{"x":0.63684,"y":0.55379,"w":0.02251,"h":0.01952,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"O R"},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.58259,"w":0.15502,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2. Sum and Difference:"},{"x":0.41127,"y":0.61006,"w":0.16176,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"(ƒ(x, y) { g(x, y)) dA ="},{"x":0.60989,"y":0.61006,"w":0.08485,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(x, y) dA {"},{"x":0.73161,"y":0.61073,"w":0.06391,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,